steps.body:
A Map remembers insertion order, so loops visit entries in the order keys were first added. Updating a value keeps its key in place, but deleting a key and setting it again moves it to the end. Loop with `for...of` and unpack each `[key, value]` pair, or use `forEach`, whose callback gets the **value first**, then the key:

```javascript
const ages = new Map([["ada", 36], ["alan", 41]]);
for (const [name, age] of ages) {
  console.log(`${name} is ${age}`);
}
ages.forEach((age, name) => console.log(`${name}: ${age}`));
```

`map.keys()`, `map.values()`, and `map.entries()` return iterators, not arrays. In Node 20 they have no `length`, `map`, or `join`, so `ages.keys().join(", ")` throws a TypeError. Spread them into an array first: `[...ages.keys()].join(", ")`. `[...map]` gives an array of `[key, value]` pairs and `Array.from(ages.keys())` works too.

